{"execution_score":6,"aesthetic_score":6,"feedback":"The Twombly palette (graphite, blood red, umber on cream) is well-chosen and restrained, and the chalk-edge fragment shader is a nice touch. However, the piece is fundamentally a head scan with wiggly displacement — the 'gestural scribble' reads as jitter on a recognizable portrait, not as Twombly's actual practice of illegible mark-making where the scribble IS the subject. The trail lines are a good instinct but at 800 trails on a 55k-point head they get lost rather than reading as autonomous gestural marks. A stronger execution would dissolve the head silhouette more aggressively — let clusters of particles break free into looping calligraphic strokes that only loosely cohere into a face, so the tension between illegibility and recognition mirrors Twombly's tension between writing and drawing."}
