{"execution_score":6,"aesthetic_score":6,"feedback":"The Twombly brief asks for scribble-as-handwriting and smeared chalk, but the piece reads more as a head dissolving into a particle cloud than as gestural calligraphic marks — the looping sine/cos paths produce uniform orbits, not the irregular, pressure-varying strokes Twombly's work demands. Replace the symmetric trig loops with polyline trail geometry or additive line segments that vary in width and opacity along their length, mimicking actual drawn marks. The palette is well-chosen (graphite, blood, umber, cream) but the uniform point-sprite rendering flattens it into a haze; elongated, directional sprites aligned to velocity would better evoke chalk drag. The head substrate is recognizable but risks the culled head-scan pattern — lean harder into illegibility by letting more clusters fully detach and trace autonomous scribble paths rather than hovering near the surface."}
